r a  youth or women's shelter  but they never know what food will be  donated so they're forced to improvise  with some cinnamon where would i be  oh i got it right you got cinnamon sir  yeah just as chef keith did when he won  an episode of chop canada  so that means you chef keith [ __ ] are  our chopped canada champion and you're  gonna make a lot of kids happy because  you just won ten thousand dollars  congratulations  fantastic thank you very much in true to  form he gave his students  extra motivation to cheer him on they  did because  uh i i said when i went on the show that  i would uh  use if i won that i would use the money  to uh  help fund a trip to europe and so uh  that's what i did i took the ten  thousand dollar prize and  we went to spain and france with the  students with the students  yeah so  these are our chefs and this was their  challenge  it all started with keith giving me a  call and saying can you come out  to humber college they've got a lot of  food there classes have stopped  they want to donate it as one of the  larger colleges in the gta  humber buys a lot of food for its  culinary program and cafeterias  but with everything shut down in march  2020 it had  fifty thousand dollars worth of fresh  food and no one to eat it  until the two chefs contacted food banks  in mississauga  which were experiencing a spike in users  we saw  a a pretty quick stiff increase of  17 to 24 percent and then year to date  we're seeing at times we see up to 30  percent  numbers like that are hard for any food  bank to swallow but the heartburn really  begins when you consider they rely  almost exclusively  on community donations and that was  about to dry up for peter costello  of mississauga's eden food for change  because his two  biggest suppliers schools and churches  were shut down  churches and and faith groups are are  suffering  as well and so where they traditionally  were good supporters of us  they tried to but they could they  couldn't they couldn't do it and that  no longer with the schools operating uh  could we  get with get support from say a toonie  tuesday kind of  event and things like that so the two  chefs had pretty good timing  but there was a problem all that food  from humber as well as more donated by  hotels and industry suppliers  was originally intended to be used in  weddings and large events  all of it fresh and perishable the clock  was ticking  and being the chef that they are wasting  food of that quality and in that  quantity  was never going to happen  to me it's almost criminal to be  throwing away good food when people are  they've lost their jobs they're  struggling money-wise and and  to find food for their families it  should not be going to landfill  and this is where it gets a little crazy  because they had inadvertently found an  overlooked element of food insecurity  shelters and food banks were still  offering baskets filled with  non-perishables  but with their kitchens closed they  could no longer offer prepared meals for  seniors and people with disabilities  who can't cook on their own  so chefs john and keith along with their  wives turned on their stoves  rolled up their sleeves and went to work  preparing full meals and desserts  from their home kitchens how many hours  a day were you in the kitchen  cooking oh probably probably  10 or 12 hours a day it was non-stop it  was from morning till night  six seven days a week yeah it was  non-stop so we're probably up at about  40 to 50  000 you know meals that we've sent out  to  shelters and food banks between four  people between four people  that doesn't seem remarkable at all  words spread about what they were doing  and soon a few carloads of food  would become truckloads chef's warehouse  along with other suppliers that sell  food in bulk to restaurants  had skids of product it could either  give away  or throw out sales manager lee bagley  says  there was almost too much i was uh  i guess optimistic that they could be  put to use but i realistically  knowing that he was going to be doing  them out of his home kitchen it was  seen to be a a big dream that they could  uh use them all three times a week i  fill up the truck and there's  there's got to be 500 to 1000 worth of  food in that truck ever  three times a week it's definitely  helped me  sleep better at night knowing that it's  uh it's been  gone to a great cause  [Music]  try and repeat the garnish with the  flavor  it's safe to say their passion for  feeding people evolved into a mission  which they estimate has saved upwards of  a million dollars worth of food  they gave their time and energy and with  only a handful of monetary donations  they shouldered almost all of the extra  costs themselves  from an outside perspective you could  say they gave  more than they received you couldn't be  more wrong  you've changed yeah i've changed i've  changed  in a good way yeah in a good way yeah  i haven't focused on all the stuff that  i can't do because of the pandemic  i focused on what i can do  because of the pandemic and what i can  do is do what i love to do  cook and share my food with people

